            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="per-25g" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Cost per Serving and per 25 g, Worked Out
          </h2>

          <p>
            Sticker price tells you almost nothing about protein powder, because tubs differ in size and servings differ in protein. The number that lets you compare any two powders, or a powder and a food, is cost per 25 g of protein. The method takes thirty seconds: multiply grams of protein per serving by servings per container, divide the price by that total, then multiply by 25. Our <a href="/tools/protein-cost-calculator/">protein cost calculator</a> does it for you if you would rather not reach for your phone's calculator in the aisle.
          </p>

          <p>
            Start with the 2 lb tub. Elevation's label gives 30 g of protein per serving, and reports of the 2 lb tub put it at about 18 to 21 servings depending on which label version you read. That is 540 to 630 g of protein per tub. At $20, the price per gram is about $0.032 to $0.037, which works out to roughly $0.80 to $0.93 per 25 g of protein, or about $0.95 to $1.11 per full 30 g serving.
          </p>

          <p>
            Now the 24 oz tub. If the serving size stays the same, a 24 oz tub holds roughly 14 to 15 servings, or about 420 to 450 g of protein. At $19.79 that is about $1.10 to $1.18 per 25 g of protein, or around $1.32 to $1.41 per serving. The smaller tub is still reasonable value. It is simply no longer the standout, and the difference adds up over a year of daily shakes.
          </p>

          <p>
            Translate that into a monthly budget and the gap is clearer. One 30 g shake a day for 30 days costs roughly $29 to $33 from 2 lb tubs, which means buying about one and a half tubs a month. From 24 oz tubs, the same habit costs roughly $40 to $42, a little over two tubs a month. Over twelve months, that is a difference of somewhere around $100 to $150 for exactly the same amount of protein.
          </p>

