                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Elevation 2 lb</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About $20</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">18-21</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About $0.95-1.11</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About $0.80-0.93</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Elevation 24 oz</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$19.79</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">14-15</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About $1.32-1.41</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About $1.10-1.18</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            These are estimates built from the label figures reported by reviewers and nutrition databases and the prices we found in early November 2026. Your store's tag may differ, and Aldi has adjusted formulas over the years. If the servings per container on your tub is different from the range above, plug your own numbers into the formula; it takes less time than reading this paragraph. Our sister guide on <a href="/how-many-servings-in-aldi-protein-powder/">how many servings are in Aldi protein powder</a> goes through the scoop sizes in more detail.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="vs-food" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Aldi Protein Powder vs Eggs, Chicken, Milk and Yogurt
          </h2>

          <p>
            Protein powder is a convenience food, so the fair question is not only whether Elevation beats other powders but whether it beats ordinary groceries. The figures below use broad, approximate US grocery price ranges, which vary a lot by store, region and season, and standard protein values from nutrition labels. Treat them as a rough map rather than a quote, and rerun the numbers with your own receipts if you want precision.
          </p>

          <p>
            Chicken breast is the benchmark for cheap complete protein. Raw boneless, skinless breast has about 22 to 23 g of protein per 100 g, so a pound gives roughly 100 g of protein. At $2 to $4.50 a pound, that is about $0.50 to $1.15 per 25 g, which means bulk chicken on sale beats Elevation, while full-price chicken from a smaller pack costs about the same. Chicken also needs cooking, storage and time that a scoop does not.
          </p>

          <p>
            Milk is the quiet bargain. A cup of cow's milk has about 8 g of protein, so a gallon gives roughly 128 g. At $3 to $4.50 a gallon, that is about $0.60 to $0.90 per 25 g, level with or cheaper than the 2 lb Elevation tub, with calcium thrown in. Eggs are more volatile. A large egg has about 6 g of protein, so a dozen gives about 72 g; at $2.50 to $5 a dozen, that is roughly $0.85 to $1.75 per 25 g, depending heavily on where egg prices are that month.
          </p>

          <p>
            Plain nonfat Greek yogurt has roughly 10 g of protein per 100 g, so a 32 oz tub holds about 90 g of protein. At $4 to $6 a tub, that comes to about $1.10 to $1.65 per 25 g, more than the 2 lb Elevation tub. Canned tuna typically gives around 20 to 25 g of protein per drained 5 oz can, and at $1 to $1.80 a can that is roughly $1.00 to $2.00 per 25 g. Both are good foods, but neither is cheaper protein than Elevation in the larger tub.
          </p>

