                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Canned tuna</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$1-1.80 / can</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">20-25 g / can</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$1.00-2.00</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            The takeaway is that the 2 lb Elevation tub sits in the same band as the cheapest whole-food protein, and the 24 oz tub sits with yogurt and eggs. Food still matters more than powder: chicken, eggs, milk and yogurt bring vitamins, minerals and satiety a shake cannot. Use Elevation to fill the gap on busy days, not as a replacement for meals. Our guide to <a href="/how-much-protein-do-i-need/">how much protein you need</a> helps you size that gap honestly.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="vs-brands" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Aldi vs the Name-Brand Wheys
          </h2>

          <p>
            Against other powders, the 2 lb Elevation tub is hard to beat. In our <a href="/best-aldi-protein-powder/">best Aldi protein powder</a> guide we compared it with five widely sold alternatives using the label figures each brand publishes and the prices we found in early November 2026. Only one, a 5 lb bag of Nutricost unflavored whey concentrate, consistently matched it, and that one comes without flavor.
          </p>

          <p>
            Body Fortress Super Advanced Whey is Elevation's closest twin, with 30 g of protein in a 45 g scoop. The 1.78 lb tub holds about 18 servings and was $25.98 at Walmart when we checked. That is 540 g of protein, or about $1.20 per 25 g and $1.44 per serving. Premier Protein 100% Whey, another 30 g powder, was $29.99 for about 17 servings at Target, which is roughly $1.47 per 25 g and $1.76 per serving, though Amazon is often cheaper.
          </p>

          <p>
            Optimum Nutrition Gold Standard 100% Whey has a smaller 24 g serving, and in the 5 lb tub it typically runs about $1.10 to $1.45 per 25 g, a little more than the 2 lb Elevation tub and dramatically less on sale. Nutricost Whey Protein Concentrate lands around $0.85 to $1.05 per 25 g at the Amazon prices reported in deal posts this year, and lower with coupons. Orgain's plant powder, with only 21 g per two scoops, costs roughly $1.60 to $2.00 per 25 g.
          </p>

