                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Orgain Organic Plant Protein</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">21 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$1.60-2.00</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Amazon, Costco</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Notice where the 24 oz Elevation tub falls. At around $1.10 to $1.18 per 25 g, it is essentially tied with Gold Standard in the 5 lb tub and only a few cents ahead of Body Fortress. At that point, the name brands start winning on things Elevation does not offer: more flavors, a leaner scoop, published banned-substance testing claims or NSF Contents Certified status. Price alone no longer settles it.
          </p>

          <p>
            Warehouse clubs are the other comparison worth making. Costco's Kirkland whey is cheaper per gram than a 24 oz Elevation tub in many cases, and our sister guide comparing <a href="/aldi-vs-costco-protein-powder/">Aldi vs Costco protein powder</a> works through that matchup gram for gram, including the membership fee most comparisons forget.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="online-price" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Shelf Price, App Price and Reseller Price
          </h2>

          <p>
            Aldi's online grocery service runs through a delivery partner, and the prices shown there can be higher than the tag in your store. That is common across grocers that outsource delivery, and Aldi says as much in its own shopping pages. Add delivery fees, service fees and a tip, and a $19.79 tub ordered for delivery can cost meaningfully more than the same tub picked off the shelf, which can quietly erase its advantage over a name brand.
          </p>

          <p>
            The fix is to use the app or aldi.us as a stock check rather than a price check. Select your store, see whether Elevation shows as available, and then compare cost per 25 g using the price you actually pay at the register. If you do order for delivery, fold the fees into the price before running the formula. Spread across a larger basket, the fees matter less; on a single tub, they matter a lot.
          </p>

          <p>
            Resellers are the bigger trap. Elevation is not sold by Aldi on Amazon, but third-party sellers sometimes list Elevation tubs on marketplace sites at a large markup. Once you add the markup and shipping, a resold tub can cost $1.50 or more per 25 g, worse than buying Body Fortress, Premier or Gold Standard directly. There is no reason to pay a premium for a store brand whose only real selling point is that it is cheap.
          </p>

          <p>
            Watch for older listings too. Aldi's site and third-party nutrition databases still carry entries for discontinued sizes and formula versions, some with different serving counts. If a price looks too good, check the size and the date of the listing before you build a budget around it. When in doubt, the tub in your hand is the only source that counts.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Price the tub you can actually buy.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Use the Aldi app to see whether your store has Elevation, then run the cost per 25 g with the shelf price and the net weight printed on the tub. Delivery fees and reseller markups can turn the cheapest whey in America into an ordinary one.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="not-a-deal" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. When Elevation Stops Being a Deal
          </h2>

          <p>
            Elevation is a good deal when three things are true: you are buying the 2 lb tub, you are paying shelf price, and you actually like the taste. Take away any one of those and its advantage shrinks fast. A tub you only half finish because you dislike the flavor is the most expensive protein powder you can buy, whatever the tag says, and that applies to every brand, not just Aldi's.
          </p>

          <p>
            The size question comes first. At roughly $1.10 to $1.18 per 25 g, the 24 oz tub costs about a third more per gram than the 2 lb tub did at the same price. That is still fair, but it puts Elevation in the same band as Body Fortress at Walmart, Gold Standard in the 5 lb tub and Premier on sale. Once prices are that close, it is worth paying a few cents more for whichever powder suits you better.
          </p>

          <p>
            Certification is the second. If you compete in a drug-tested sport, Elevation is not a deal at any price, because we found no NSF Certified for Sport or Informed Sport listing for it. A certified powder costs more per gram, but it is the only way to reduce the risk of a contaminated lot. The same logic applies if you want published heavy-metal testing: Elevation has none we could find, so you are paying less partly because you are getting less assurance.
          </p>

          <p>
            Diet is the third. Elevation is a dairy whey blend sweetened with sucralose and acesulfame potassium. If you avoid dairy, artificial sweeteners or both, its price is irrelevant; Orgain or an unflavored powder will cost more per gram but is the one you will actually drink. Our honest look at <a href="/is-elevation-protein-powder-good/">whether Elevation protein powder is any good</a> covers the label trade-offs in full.
          </p>

          <p>
            Health caveat, briefly. Protein powder is food, not medicine, and nothing here is medical advice. If you have kidney disease, are pregnant, have a milk allergy or take medication that affects appetite or digestion, ask a doctor or registered dietitian about your protein target before adding a daily shake, cheap or not.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="spend-less" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. How to Spend Less per Gram of Protein
          </h2>

          <p>
            The single best move is buying the 2 lb tub whenever your store has it. If shelves carry both sizes, the larger one is the better buy by a wide margin, and an unopened whey tub keeps for many months stored somewhere cool and dry. Check the best-by date, buy two if you drink a shake most days, and you lock in the lower price per gram until the next restock.
          </p>

          <p>
            The second move is mixing with milk instead of water if you drink milk anyway. A cup of milk adds about 8 g of protein for roughly 20 to 30 cents at the gallon prices above, which means a shake made with half a serving of powder and a cup of milk gives about 23 g of protein for less than a full scoop with water. It also tastes creamier, which matters if you find Elevation thin.
          </p>

          <p>
            Third, lean on the Aldi guarantee rather than buying a cheap sample from a reseller. Aldi has long let shoppers return a product they are unhappy with for a refund or replacement; check your store's current terms. That means the risk of trying a new flavor is small, and you never need to pay a marketplace premium just to find out whether you like it.
          </p>

          <p>
            Fourth, know your fallback. If your Aldi is out or has only the 24 oz tub, a 5 lb bag of Nutricost unflavored whey bought with a coupon can undercut Elevation, and Gold Standard on a good sale often matches it with more flavors and a leaner scoop. Our guide to the <a href="/best-cheap-protein-powder/">best cheap protein powder</a> keeps a running list of the lowest cost-per-gram options outside Aldi.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, do not buy more protein than you need. Most adults get a large share of their protein from meals, and a shake only needs to fill the gap. If one 30 g serving gets you there, you will finish a 2 lb tub in about three weeks; if half a serving does, the same tub lasts six. The cheapest gram of protein is the one you were going to eat anyway at dinner.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Comparing tub prices instead of cost per 25 g</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A $19.79 tub sounds the same as a $20 tub, but if one is 24 oz and the other 2 lb, they are not the same deal. Always divide price by total protein and multiply by 25. That is the only number that lets you compare Elevation fairly with a name brand or a food.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming Aldi's website price is the shelf price</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Aldi's online orders run through a delivery partner, and listed prices can be higher than the tag in store. Fees and tips push the real cost higher still. Use the website to check stock, then calculate value with the register price, not the screen price.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Paying a reseller premium for Elevation</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Marketplace listings for Elevation can carry markups and shipping that push the cost past $1.50 per 25 g. At that point Body Fortress, Premier or Gold Standard bought directly are cheaper. Elevation is only worth buying at Aldi's own price.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring serving size when comparing brands</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Elevation lists 30 g of protein per serving, Gold Standard 24 g and Orgain 21 g. Cost per serving therefore overstates how expensive the bigger scoops are. Compare per 25 g of protein so a large serving and a small one are judged on the same terms.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying a big stash of a flavor you have not tried</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A cheap tub you do not drink is wasted money. Buy one tub of a new flavor first, and use Aldi's return guarantee if you dislike it. Stock up only once you know it is a shake you will finish, then buy the 2 lb size when it is available.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="faq-list">
            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>How much is Aldi protein powder?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Elevation by Millville whey has been sold at roughly $20 for a 2 lb tub, and when we checked aldi.us in early November 2026 the newest listing was a 24 oz tub at $19.79. Prices vary by store, and Aldi's online prices can be higher than the shelf tag, so check the price and net weight in your store.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>How much does Aldi protein powder cost per serving?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  About $0.95 to $1.11 per 30 g serving in the 2 lb tub, assuming 18 to 21 servings at about $20. In the 24 oz tub at $19.79, with roughly 14 to 15 servings, it is about $1.32 to $1.41 per serving. Your tub's servings per container may differ slightly, so check the label.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is Aldi protein powder cheaper than name brands?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  In the 2 lb tub, usually yes. At roughly $0.80 to $0.93 per 25 g of protein it beats Body Fortress, Premier and most Gold Standard prices, and only bulk unflavored whey such as Nutricost matches it. The 24 oz tub, at about $1.10 to $1.18 per 25 g, is roughly level with name brands on sale.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is protein powder cheaper than chicken or eggs?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Sometimes. Chicken breast on sale, at $2 to $3 a pound, gives cheaper protein than any powder, and milk is level with the 2 lb Elevation tub. Eggs, Greek yogurt and canned tuna generally cost more per 25 g of protein than the larger Elevation tub, though prices swing by region and season.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Does Aldi put protein powder on sale?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Rarely. Elevation tends to stay at the same shelf price week to week, without the coupons and promotions name brands run. That makes its price steady rather than spectacular. Name brands can undercut it briefly during big sales or with Subscribe and Save, so compare cost per 25 g when you see a deal.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Why did Aldi protein powder get more expensive?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  The tag barely changed, but newer aldi.us listings show a 24 oz tub at $19.79 where the 2 lb tub used to sell for about $20. If the serving size is unchanged, that is roughly a third more per gram of protein. We could not confirm whether every store has switched, so check the net weight.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Can I buy Aldi protein powder online?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Through Aldi's own online grocery service, which runs on a delivery partner, yes, where your store offers it, but listed prices can be higher than in store and fees apply. Elevation is not sold by Aldi on Amazon. Third-party resellers sometimes list it at a markup, which usually makes a name brand cheaper.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

          </div>
